s the button; Chat cannot do any of these itself.\n\n**Bringing in another of your articles.** Type <kbd>@</kbd> and start typing a title to pick one of\nyour own articles, archived ones included; it goes into a **Bringing in** row above the box, and ×\ntakes it out again. Once you send, that conversation has the whole of the other article to hand,\nand a line at the top says **Also reading**. When an answer relies on it, the short code shows that\narticle's name with an arrow, and opens it at the paragraph. Up to three articles can be brought\ninto one conversation, each up to about 120,000 characters (a long book chapter is more); a longer\none is refused, and your question stays in the box. To compare with a file, add it to your shelf\nfirst, then bring it in once it is ready.\n\n**The guide.** The **Guide** row pinned at the top of the list, with a compass, is a conversation about how to read\nthis article with Spideryarn, rather than about what it says. A new article opens on it when there\nis room beside the text, with a greeting that is ours and costs nothing; nothing is asked of the AI\nuntil you send something. When the guide suggests a mode or a passage, it gives you a button and\nsays what pressing it will do; it never opens anything or moves you by itself. To help you decide\nhow to read the piece, it can search the web for what others have said about it, and read one web\npage an answer, such as one you paste in; a question about the subject itself, it hands to a new\nChat. Its\ngreeting asks why you are reading the piece: answer in the box below as you would any message. When\nyou say why, or something about yourself, the guide offers to save it, as close to your own words\nas it can, under its answer: **Save as why you're reading** or **Save to About you**. Nothing is\nsaved until you press, unless the words are exactly ones you typed in that message, or one whole\nsentence of it with only the spelling tidied (*I'm* written *I am*, a capital, a full s